<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamesna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamesna is the historical name of the Atlantic coastal plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> between the Bou Regreg river at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> and the Oum Er-Rbia river. From the 8th century it was the heartland of the Barghawata, whose state was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>. The Almohads and later dynasties resettled it with Arab tribes, and it corresponds largely to the modern Chaouia region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamesna.jpg" alt="Tamesna" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Omar-toons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barghouata_Carte.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name is written Tamesna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and Tāmasnā in Arabic. The 16th-century Andalusian author <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-africanus">Leo Africanus</a>, in his Description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, counted Temesna among the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and placed it between the river Ommirabih in the west and the Buragrag in the east, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> to the south and the ocean to the north. He described it as a plain about fourscore miles long and threescore broad, once the chief of the seven provinces, with forty great towns and three hundred castles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its indigenous people were the Barghwata. Leo tells that in the year 323 of the Hegeira a man he calls Chemim, son of Mennal, a self-proclaimed prophet, freed the province from tribute and made a pact with the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> that left him Temesna, which he ruled for about thirty-five years and his successors for nearly a century more.</p><p>In Leo's account the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamtuna">Lamtuna</a> king who had built Maroco then sent religious teachers to win the province over; its people had them murdered and raised an army of fifty thousand men. The king crossed the Ommirabih, put the inhabitants to the sword and razed their towns in eight days, while the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> cut off those who had fled north near the Buragrag, so that only a small remnant survived and the land was left to wild beasts.</p><p>About 150 years later, Leo writes, King Mansor brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> and settled them there; they held it for fifty years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamtuna">Lamtuna</a> drove them out. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a> then granted Tamasna to the Zenata and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawwara">Hawwara</a> confederations, who came to dominate the province and, in Leo's day, could field sixty thousand horsemen and held two hundred castles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tamasna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…with only nominal power over the rest, al-Qasim Tangier, 'Umar the Sanhaja and Ghumara of the Rif, Isa the <b>Tamesna</b> coast around Chellah, Yahya Hisn Dai, Hamza Walili and 'Ubayd Allah the south with the Lamta tribes and Tamdult, while Agadir, later Tlemcen, remained with the Sulaymanid cousins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Barghawata emerged in the <b>Tamesna</b> plain between Salé and Safi after the Berber revolt of 740 against the Umayyad Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/state/barghawata">Barghawata</a></li></ul></section>
